        for unrelated reasons). Do **not** set a module-level "refused" flag as the
        primary control.
        **Deliberate answer:** control-flow short-circuit. refuseBooleanSpaceForm and
        originalRun are sequential statements inside the same runGraceCommand
        operation callback. When refuse throws, runGraceCommand catches, renders,
        sets process.exitCode from the error, and returns — originalRun is never
        reached because the throw aborted the callback. Proof: existing
        "ran === false" counterweight stays, but after the change the wrapper **does
        not rethrow** (runGraceCommand swallows). Update unit tests that currently
        expect cmd.run to reject: they must observe ran===false, process.exitCode
        set, and (json mode) a valid envelope on stdout instead of a rejection.

        Nested runGraceCommand: many leaves already wrap their bodies in
        runGraceCommand / runQueryCommand. Nesting is harmless because the inner
        call also swallows — outer operation completes after one render. Do not
        rewrite leaf success paths (NonGoal).

      Trap 2 — two JSON conventions (measured at plan authoring; re-check at execute):
        --format only:        gate, lint, cursor, graph, review, doctor
        --format and --json:  status, module, context, file, verification

        resolveErrorFormat(args) used by the wrapper (same predicate the leaves
        already use for errorFormat):

